[b]Oscillator Cycle Statistics[/b] is a statistics engine for bounded oscillators — RSI, Stochastic RSI, Stochastic, normalized Williams %R, MFI, CCI, WaveTrend, DeMarker, RVI, EOM, and Klinger. It does not generate trade signals. Instead it tracks the full cycle from one extreme (overbought/oversold) to the opposite extreme and reports how often that move historically completed under comparable conditions. Normalized Williams %R is explicitly identified as equivalent to Stochastic %K. EOM and Klinger require real volume data — on zero-volume CFD/index feeds they sit at a constant neutral value and never produce a cycle.

[b]What it does[/b]

The indicator runs an explicit state machine on the chosen oscillator: a cycle starts on fresh entry into Overbought or Oversold, becomes "armed" once the oscillator leaves that zone, and is resolved as a success (opposite extreme reached), a failure (origin extreme revisited first), or a timeout. Every completed cycle is stored with its direction, outcome, starting market regime, duration, maximum progress reached, and price MFE/MAE in ATR.

[b]Statistics[/b]

[list]
[*][b]Progress- and Age-Conditioned Probability[/b] — uses time-consistent 10-percentage-point progress buckets and matches only cycles that had reached the bucket by the queried age and remained unresolved beyond it
[*][b]Regime-Conditioned Probability[/b] — same query, narrowed to cycles that started in the same market regime (Bull Trend / Bear Trend / Range)
[*][b]Recent / Medium / Long Lookback[/b] — compares near-term cycle behavior against the longer-run average to expose drift
[*][b]Descriptive Wilson Interval[/b] — paired with an explicit minimum-sample status; consecutive cycles can be dependent, so it is not presented as a guaranteed confidence band
[*][b]Directional Base Rates[/b] — shows OB → OS and OS → OB separately to expose asymmetry
[*][b]Median Time-to-Target[/b] and origin-based price MFE/MAE in ATR, split between successful and failed cycles
[*][b]Walk-forward Reliability Table[/b] — average forecast, observed rate, error, and sample size per bucket, restricted to the current Medium lookback
[*][b]Brier Scores[/b] — base forecast quality, a fair base/regime comparison on the same cycles, and a naive direction-base-rate Brier Skill Score so the model has an actual floor to beat
[*][b]Cycle/Evidence Readout[/b] — describes direction, lifecycle stage, completion evidence, and limits without translating oscillator completion into trade permission
[/list]

[b]Display[/b]

Cycle lines drawn directly on the oscillator are the primary read: a straight royal-blue line to the opposite rail for a completed success (deliberately not red/green, since a cycle's own outcome isn't a price direction call). A reverted failure draws a gray V-shape — origin, deepest point reached, back to origin — instead of a straight line, which would land on the exact same rail it started from and disappear against the gridline; a timeout draws a straight gray line to wherever the oscillator actually was. Width scales with how far the cycle actually got, and a live line tracks the active cycle to the current bar with a target marker and a dashed Expected Target Time projection. Recent cycles stay full color; older ones fade so the active cycle isn't visually competing with history. The live label leads with Edge — Conditional completion minus the direction's plain base rate — plus a state word (LOW DATA / STALLED / UNCERTAIN / LATE / OPPORTUNITY / WATCH / WAIT), instead of a raw percentage that can look strong on a single-digit sample. The label uses the exact same sample-size, Wilson-interval-width, and progress-zone gates as the Opportunity marker itself, so it can never claim OPPORTUNITY in a situation the marker would refuse to fire on. That marker fires at most once per cycle, only when Edge, sample size, interval width, and an early-enough progress zone all clear their thresholds together. Start and Success/Failure markers are off by default — outcome is already visible in the completed line's color and style. The default `Visual` dashboard mode reduces the table to a one-line confirmation tag, since the chart itself carries the story; `Compact` adds a decision-relevant two-column table, and `Research` restores the full statistics dashboard. Fresh entry remains explicitly not validated because current price excursion starts at the cycle origin rather than at a tradable decision checkpoint. Lifecycle labels are descriptive progress bands based on the cycle's current position, not entry gates. The separate Reliability table is off by default and collapses to one collection-status row until enough observations exist.

[b]Scope[/b]

This version deliberately omits divergence detection, volume/ADX confirmation, a composite score, and trading signals. Oscillator completion is not treated as evidence of trade profitability. The goal is to establish whether the cycle forecast is stable before validating price outcomes from realistic entry checkpoints or layering additional conditions on top.

